{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Thus shall you bless the children of Israel.</b> The blessings were placed adjacent to the passage of the nazirite, because wine fixes blessing for itself but not for others — and on the contrary, it brought weeping and curse to the world, according to the one who said that the Tree of Knowledge was a vine (Sanhedrin 70a); and so it caused curse to Adam and Eve, and to the seed of Noah, and to Lot. And the word ״כֹּה״ refers to the passage above, to say that the priest who blesses must not be drunken with wine when he stands to bless Israel, for he must recite the blessings exactly as written, ״אָמוֹר לָהֶם״, and anyone who cannot speak before the king is called drunk — therefore they must be like nazirites. And with the word ״כֹּה״ He sealed all the blessings, that they should be patterned after the blessing of Abraham, who was blessed with ״כֹּה״, as it says (Genesis 15:5) ״כֹּה יִהְיֶה זַרְעֶךָ״. Therefore Balaam wanted to nullify from Israel the blessing of ״כֹּה״, as it says (Numbers 23:15): ״הִתְיַצֵּב כֹּה עַל עֹלָתֶךָ וְאָנֹכִי אִקָּרֶה כֹּה״, and they said in the Midrash: You, through your offerings, would nullify the blessing of ״כֹּה יִהְיֶה זַרְעֶךָ״, and I would nullify the blessing of ״כֹּה תְבָרְכוּ״, etc." "And with this I have seen fit to explain what is concluded in Rabboti (Bamidbar Rabbah 20:7) on the verse ״אוּלַי אוּכַל נַכֶּה בּוֹ״ (ibid. 22:6): just as one subtracts from a se'ah a twenty-fourth portion, so he sought to subtract from Israel a twenty-fourth portion of sixty myriads, that is, twenty-five thousand. And one may ask: what advantage would he have in all his labor if he subtracts twenty-five thousand from sixty myriads? And furthermore, why did specifically twenty-four thousand fall? Rather, certainly he sought nothing but to nullify from Israel the blessing of ״כֹּה״, and he reckoned that the fall of twenty-five thousand would strike at the blessing of ״כֹּה״, and through the nullification of this blessing he would have room then to curse them. But he did not succeed, for he was unable against them except by the number twenty-four up to the number twenty-five and not inclusive — for the blessing of ״כֹּה תְבָרְכוּ״ stood for them, so that the curse would not take effect further, and this is a precious interpretation." "And perhaps the number כֹּה is in the merit of the twenty-four priestly gifts, and together with the blessing of the priests, behold there are twenty-five priestly gifts — through their merit the blessing rests upon Israel. And if you ask: what benefit do the priests have from this blessing, that it should be counted among all the priestly gifts? Scripture comes to teach: (Genesis 12:3) ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ״ — if so, through this blessing they too receive blessing (see Ḥullin 49a; 'and I will bless them' — it was taught, Rabbi Yishmael says, etc.: the priests bless Israel and the Holy One, blessed be He, blesses the priests). And furthermore, the text of the blessings is ״יְבָרֶכְךָ ה׳״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted it (Bamidbar Rabbah 11:5) as referring to possessions, and this blessing returns to the priests as well, for when Israel have abundant possessions proportionate to their population, the priestly gifts of terumot and tithes will multiply — for if not, from where would they take them: from the empty threshing floor or from the empty winepress?" "<b>Say to them.</b> From here our Rabbis of blessed memory learned (Sifrei 6:143) that the ḥazzan recites to the priests word by word the text of the blessings. And the reason for this is that the ḥazzan is the conduit who draws the flow of the channel from the source of blessings first and pours it down upon the heads of the priests so that it may rest — for he first says to the priest ״יְבָרֶכְךָ ה׳״, in order to make the priest first into a full vessel, overflowing with the blessing of HaShem, and afterward, when the priest says to Israel ״יְבָרֶכְךָ ה׳״, he pours from a full vessel onto an empty one. But if the priest were not blessed first, he would be pouring from an empty vessel into an empty vessel. And with this one may settle what is written (Genesis 12:3): ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶךָ וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ אָאֹר״ — and it should have said ״וְאָאֹר מְקַלֶּלְךָ״ in the manner that it said ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶךָ״; rather, it wanted to say that the priest who blesses Israel — I bless him first, in order to make the priest a full and overflowing vessel, and he pours from a full vessel of HaShem's blessing upon Israel. But with a curse it is not appropriate to say thus; rather, after he curses them, I will curse him, and not before. Therefore it says ״וְשָׂמוּ אֶת שְׁמִי עַל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַאֲנִי אֲבָרְכֵם״ — first (see Ḥullin 49a), by means of the ḥazzan saying to the priest first ״יְבָרֶכְךָ ה׳״."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:50.609427094Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}